\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Layer 4 drawn rather than argued. The released head asked for three objects emits three place points, $14.5$ and $13.0$\,cm from the basket. The basket is \emph  {not} fixed --- it is re-placed every episode, with a placement radius of $1.83$\,cm averaged over the suite's ten tasks --- so a memorised place point is wrong by $14$\,cm against a target that moves by under $2$, and $14$\,cm is enough to release a grocery over open table. \textbf  {Only radial distance is measured}: the angles are chosen for legibility and carry no information, which is why no coordinate grid is drawn.}}{11}{figure.caption.39}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:placepoint}{{7}{11}{Layer 4 drawn rather than argued. The released head asked for three objects emits three place points, $14.5$ and $13.0$\,cm from the basket. The basket is \emph {not} fixed --- it is re-placed every episode, with a placement radius of $1.83$\,cm averaged over the suite's ten tasks --- so a memorised place point is wrong by $14$\,cm against a target that moves by under $2$, and $14$\,cm is enough to release a grocery over open table. \textbf {Only radial distance is measured}: the angles are chosen for legibility and carry no information, which is why no coordinate grid is drawn}{figure.caption.39}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Sweeping the placement-randomisation radius on the same draws. Randomisation robustness is a curve, and at $n{=}10$ this curve is not even monotone --- the undisplaced cell sits \emph  {below} $\pm 2$\,cm, which cannot be a real benefit of displacement. Reporting any single radius as ``passes randomisation'' would quote a number this sample does not resolve, and we do not use this table to set any threshold elsewhere in the paper. $^\ddagger $Per-trial outcomes for the $\pm 2$, $\pm 6$ and $\pm 8$ rows are in \texttt  {results/pod\_cells.json}; the two marked rows were measured in an earlier session and their trial records are not in the repository.}}{11}{table.caption.43}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:sweep}{{10}{11}{Sweeping the placement-randomisation radius on the same draws. Randomisation robustness is a curve, and at $n{=}10$ this curve is not even monotone --- the undisplaced cell sits \emph {below} $\pm 2$\,cm, which cannot be a real benefit of displacement. Reporting any single radius as ``passes randomisation'' would quote a number this sample does not resolve, and we do not use this table to set any threshold elsewhere in the paper. $^\ddagger $Per-trial outcomes for the $\pm 2$, $\pm 6$ and $\pm 8$ rows are in \texttt {results/pod\_cells.json}; the two marked rows were measured in an earlier session and their trial records are not in the repository}{table.caption.43}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {8.1}Probes alone cannot certify}{11}{subsection.8.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Substitution-probe attribution, in cm of movement of the predicted grasp \emph  {offset}, per input channel. The counterexample is the first two rows: v2 and v2.1 agree to within $0.73$\,cm on every channel and score $0.000$ and $0.700$ when deployed --- these are heads v2 and v2.1, an earlier pair, and their $0.700$ is not the released head's $35/50$. A probe evaluated on teacher trajectories cannot, on its own, certify off-manifold behaviour.}}{11}{table.caption.44}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:probe}{{11}{11}{Substitution-probe attribution, in cm of movement of the predicted grasp \emph {offset}, per input channel. The counterexample is the first two rows: v2 and v2.1 agree to within $0.73$\,cm on every channel and score $0.000$ and $0.700$ when deployed --- these are heads v2 and v2.1, an earlier pair, and their $0.700$ is not the released head's $35/50$. A probe evaluated on teacher trajectories cannot, on its own, certify off-manifold behaviour}{table.caption.44}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {8.2}An instrument only ever shown firing is not yet an instrument}{12}{subsection.8.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces The positive control our detection-agreement instrument had never been given. ``Same-box'' is the fraction of frames on which two objects' prompt chains select the identical box; a high rate means the grounding stage carries no object identity. Run against \emph  {every} counterpart rather than only the ones that fire, the instrument separates cleanly --- and the split is interpretable: the chains that collide are the cans and bottles, the ones that separate are the boxes. Deployed binding is blind \emph  {within} a shape class, which is the regime LIBERO-Object's groceries occupy. Small $n$ (only $4$--$6$ of $60$ frames have both chains detecting) makes these demonstrations of discrimination, not calibrated rates.}}{12}{table.caption.45}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:poscontrol}{{12}{12}{The positive control our detection-agreement instrument had never been given. ``Same-box'' is the fraction of frames on which two objects' prompt chains select the identical box; a high rate means the grounding stage carries no object identity. Run against \emph {every} counterpart rather than only the ones that fire, the instrument separates cleanly --- and the split is interpretable: the chains that collide are the cans and bottles, the ones that separate are the boxes. Deployed binding is blind \emph {within} a shape class, which is the regime LIBERO-Object's groceries occupy. Small $n$ (only $4$--$6$ of $60$ frames have both chains detecting) makes these demonstrations of discrimination, not calibrated rates}{table.caption.45}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {8.3}Certificates are joint in (head, stack), down to the renderer}{12}{subsection.8.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Changing only the OpenGL backend MuJoCo renders through --- same seed, same trials, same weights, same package versions. The aggregate is identical and $40\%$ of the individual episodes are not. Matching a published rate is therefore weak evidence of having reproduced an experiment, and any paired statistic computed across a renderer change is invalid, because pairing assumes the trials are the same trials.}}{12}{table.caption.46}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:renderer}{{13}{12}{Changing only the OpenGL backend MuJoCo renders through --- same seed, same trials, same weights, same package versions. The aggregate is identical and $40\%$ of the individual episodes are not. Matching a published rate is therefore weak evidence of having reproduced an experiment, and any paired statistic computed across a renderer change is invalid, because pairing assumes the trials are the same trials}{table.caption.46}{}}
